WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.530
Like people, languages come in families.

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:08.100
No, there aren’t such things as mommy, daddy,
and baby languages, but rather languages that

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.900
share common origins and characteristics,
based upon their evolution throughout human

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:13.900
history.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:18.940
Hi, my name is Luca from LucaLampariello.com
and today I'm going to talk about language

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:23.539
families, and how learning languages from
the same family can benefit (and occasionally

00:00:23.539 --> 00:00:25.780
harm) your language learning progress.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:28.120
So, first: “What is a language family?”

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:29.860
I’ll give you an example:

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:32.509
You might know that there is a group of languages
called the “Romance languages”.

00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:36.199
There are many, many Romance languages, but
the most well-known examples include Spanish,

00:00:36.199 --> 00:00:40.190
French, Portuguese, Romanian, Catalan, and
my native Italian.

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:44.559
Despite the fact that many people consider
French, Spanish, and Italian languages of

00:00:44.559 --> 00:00:50.039
“love”, the word “Romance” in this
case has nothing to do with love at all—instead,

00:00:50.039 --> 00:00:54.851
“Romance” means “of the Romans”, or,
more specifically, “related to the language

00:00:54.851 --> 00:00:58.160
spoken by the Romans”, which was the Latin
language.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.789
You see, once upon a time, languages like
Italian, French, and Romanian didn’t exist

00:01:02.789 --> 00:01:03.789
at all.

00:01:03.789 --> 00:01:07.820
Back in the days of the Roman Empire, the
main language spoken in what are now called

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:11.320
Italy, France, and Romania was simply Latin.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:16.409
Then one day, the Roman Empire fell, and the
Latin spoken in each of these areas began

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:19.250
to evolve, independently of other locations.

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:24.310
The variety of Latin spoken in Italy gradually
became Italian, the variety spoken in France

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:28.770
became French, the variety of Romania became
Romanian, and so on.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:33.189
So much time has passed now, in fact, that
speakers of these once-Latin languages occasionally

00:01:33.189 --> 00:01:36.549
have a hard time understanding one another,
if they can at all.

00:01:36.549 --> 00:01:41.760
Italians, for example, have great difficulty
understanding Romanian speech, especially

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.049
without the aid of writing.

00:01:43.049 --> 00:01:44.049
And vice versa.

00:01:44.049 --> 00:01:48.159
So, in a nutshell, that’s what a language
family is: a group of languages that evolved

00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:50.020
from the same ancestor language.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.939
And language families can have multiple levels,
as well.

00:01:52.939 --> 00:01:57.869
Modern Greek is not a Romance language, but
bears many similarities to Romance languages

00:01:57.869 --> 00:02:03.210
because its ancestor, Ancient Greek, was a
sister language to Latin.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:09.040
Latin and Greek (and many, many other languages)
actually evolved from a common ancestor language

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:10.190
known as Indo-European.

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:14.210
English is a descendant of Indo-European,
as well.

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:18.480
Other well-known language families include:
The Germanic languages - German, Dutch, Swedish,

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:19.960
Norwegian, Danish, Icelandic, English, etc.

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:28.300
The Slavic Languages - Russian, Polish, Czech,
Bulgarian, Macedonian, Serbo-Croatian, etc.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:31.490
The Sinitic Languages - Mandarin, Cantonese,
Shanghainese, etc.

00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:34.870
There’s a lot more than that, but I think
you get the idea.

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:37.200
So why are language families important?

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.950
Why should you, as a language learner, spend
any time thinking about how languages are

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:43.080
related to one another?

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:48.120
Language families are important because in
most cases, it is faster to learn multiple

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:53.170
languages from the same family than it is
to learn the same number of languages from

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:54.760
completely different families.

00:02:54.760 --> 00:03:00.790
In other words, knowing about language families
can make it easier for you to become a polyglot.

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:04.250
There are downsides as well, but let’s first
dive into the benefits:

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.990
1 Shared Linguistic Features Are Easier to
Master

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:10.890
In general, learning languages from the same
family is easier because languages within

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:14.900
the same family share many similar characteristics
with one another.

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:18.790
Exactly which characteristics are shared will
vary from language pair to language pair,

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:21.760
but it’s fair to say that any similarity
is possible.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:26.640
By this, I mean that languages within the
same family can share:

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.480
Vocabulary
Syntax (word order)

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:32.930
Morphology (word composition)
Grammar

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:36.020
Pronunciation
Tone/Intonation

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:37.020
and more.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:40.901
Once you’ve expended the necessary effort
to learn one language within a family, you

00:03:40.901 --> 00:03:46.980
can then choose to learn a related language
with one or more of the above shared characteristics.

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:51.440
When you learn this related language, it will
be much easier for you to master the shared

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:55.470
element, since you already did all the hard
work with your previous language.

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:56.900
Let me give you an example:

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:59.240
Polish is a Slavic language.

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:05.290
When I started learning Polish in late 2011,
I could already speak Russian, which is also

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:06.930
a Slavic language.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:11.350
Since I knew Russian well, I was already familiar
with the common features that all members

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:15.910
of the Slavic language family share, such
as the aspect of verbs, the form of words,

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:20.780
native Slavic vocabulary, syntax, and needless
to say, the case system.

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:26.010
For that reason, it was easier and faster
to learn Polish in 2011 than it was for me

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:28.280
to learn Russian in 2004.

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:33.230
Knowing Russian already meant that I could
learn a lot of Polish “for free” (that

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:35.680
is, without expending a lot of effort).

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:36.680
2.

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:39.870
You can understand other languages of the
same group without speaking them.

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:44.430
Within the same language group, there are
some languages that are closer to others,

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:45.590
and in different ways.

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:50.450
For example, Portuguese is surprisingly similar
to Spanish when written, but surprisingly

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:52.040
different when spoken.

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:56.780
In particular, the pronunciation of Iberian
(Portugal) Portuguese sounds very different

00:04:56.780 --> 00:05:01.530
from any variety of Spanish—in fact, when
I heard Iberian Portuguese for the first time,

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:03.389
I thought it sounded like Russian!

00:05:03.389 --> 00:05:08.400
On the other hand, Italian and Spanish are
structurally less similar than Spanish and

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.150
Portuguese, but share a very close pronunciation.

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:15.070
So, if you put an Italian and a Spaniard together
in the same room and forced them to speak

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:19.010
to one another without using a common language,
they would probably understand each other

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:22.970
more easily than if you repeated the same
experiment with a Portuguese person and a

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:23.970
Spaniard.

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:28.020
I was able to take advantage of this phenomenon
after I had reached a comfortable level in

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:29.020
Polish.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:32.080
Once, when I was traveling through Hungary,
I encountered a Slovak truck driver who could

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:33.490
only speak Slovak.

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:39.040
Slovak is a Slavic language, but despite that,
speakers of Slovak cannot easily understand

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:40.040
Russian.

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:45.230
So, to communicate with this person, I had
to resort to using Polish, which is a much

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:48.120
closer sister language to Slovak than Russian
is.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:51.350
Despite the fact that we were speaking two
different languages to one another, this man

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:56.450
and I succeeded quite well at communicating—in
fact, we didn’t even have to slow down our

00:05:56.450 --> 00:05:57.610
talking speed!

00:05:57.610 --> 00:06:02.110
An interesting side note to this phenomenon
is the fact that sometimes the ease or difficulty

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:05.850
of understanding a sister language depends
on which language you speak.

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:10.910
As I mentioned earlier, Portuguese and Spanish
are related, but it is often easier for Portuguese

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:14.800
people to understand Spanish people than the
other way around.

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:15.800
3.

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:18.840
Actively learning one member of a language
family can help you maintain your existing

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:21.940
level in other languages within that family.

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:24.110
Language skill is not a static thing.

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:28.760
If you learn and use a language actively and
frequently, your level in that language will

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:34.910
improve; if you don’t do that, then your
level will gradually decrease, sometimes dramatically.

00:06:34.910 --> 00:06:37.419
This is one of the main challenges that polyglots
face.

00:06:37.419 --> 00:06:42.830
To maintain their skill level in all the languages
they know, they need to find time to practice

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:43.830
those languages.

00:06:43.830 --> 00:06:47.070
And there’s only so much time in a day to
do that.

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:50.870
When you know multiple languages within the
same family, however, this challenge gets

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:51.890
a bit easier.

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:56.180
Since languages within the family share many
similar characteristics, actively practicing

00:06:56.180 --> 00:07:01.470
one language within that family can help reinforce
your existing understanding of other languages

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:05.760
in the family, even if you don’t actively
practice the others at all.

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:11.010
For example, I currently speak five Germanic
languages: German, Dutch, English, Swedish,

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:12.010
and Danish.

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:16.810
Aside from English, German is the language
of this group I speak most often (I use it

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:18.620
with my coaching clients).

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:23.340
German and Dutch are very similar, but I hardly
ever speak Dutch at all, nowadays.

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:28.230
Despite this, my frequent use of German has
allowed me to maintain a respectable level

00:07:28.230 --> 00:07:32.680
in Dutch, and allowed me to use it well on
certain rare occasions.

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:36.960
Ok, and now let’s talk about the the downside
of learning Languages from the same Family

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:41.710
So far, it may seem like learning languages
in families is something that only offers

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:42.710
advantages.

00:07:42.710 --> 00:07:45.979
However, as they say, “all that glitters
is not gold”.

00:07:45.979 --> 00:07:49.919
There is one main disadvantage to learning
languages from the same group, and it can

00:07:49.919 --> 00:07:52.560
be summed up in a single word:

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:53.560
Interference.

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:55.040
What does “interference” mean?

00:07:55.040 --> 00:08:01.340
It means mixing up and confusing the languages,
both as you learn them, and when you go to

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:02.340
use them.

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:07.040
Since languages within a family are similar,
but not the same, it is very easy to confuse

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:08.889
them as you go about your learning.

00:08:08.889 --> 00:08:13.550
Since you’ll generally always have one language
that is “stronger” and another that is

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:18.360
“weaker” in your mind, the stronger language
will often exert more influence, and make

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:22.050
it harder to “relearn” similar structures
in the new language.

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:24.330
This happened to me with Dutch and German.

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:27.630
When I started learning Dutch, I already spoke
German quite well.

00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:32.070
So, whenever I went to use a Dutch structure
that was similar to a German one, my brain

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:35.079
automatically chose the German version.

00:08:35.079 --> 00:08:39.469
This led to me speaking an awkward hybrid
of Dutch and German, rather than just Dutch,

00:08:39.469 --> 00:08:40.769
as I intended.

00:08:40.769 --> 00:08:42.439
For example, I used to say:

00:08:42.439 --> 00:08:47.010
“Als ik in Amsterdam was” (when I was
in Amsterdam) instead of “TOEN ik in Amsterdam

00:08:47.010 --> 00:08:51.970
was” because in German, “als” is the
word you use to introduce a temporal clause:

00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:55.399
“Als ich in Amsterdam war” would be the
German version.

00:08:55.399 --> 00:08:59.869
This kind of interference is inevitable at
first, but can gradually be minimized, or

00:08:59.869 --> 00:09:01.369
eliminated altogether.

00:09:01.369 --> 00:09:06.579
The best way to do this is to learn one language
in the family extremely well (to around a

00:09:06.579 --> 00:09:09.750
B2 level) before you learn another language
in the family.

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:14.259
At that point, you will have built a strong
enough “core” in the first language that

00:09:14.259 --> 00:09:18.009
the second, weaker language will be unable
to interfere much with it.

00:09:18.009 --> 00:09:22.300
Of course, as I mentioned earlier, the stronger
language will interfere with the weaker language,

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:25.970
but as you improve your skill in the weaker
language, this interference will decrease,

00:09:25.970 --> 00:09:29.990
and the two languages will grow strong and
independent of one another.

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:34.009
What happens if you don’t earn one language
well before learning another from the same

00:09:34.009 --> 00:09:35.139
family?

00:09:35.139 --> 00:09:36.430
Massive interference!

00:09:36.430 --> 00:09:41.779
Since both languages are not at a B2 level,
they are not strong enough to withstand interference

00:09:41.779 --> 00:09:42.779
from the other.

00:09:42.779 --> 00:09:45.890
It’s as if they were two young trees planted
next to one another.

00:09:45.890 --> 00:09:51.430
Small and weak, the two trees will have to
compete for the same water and air to survive.

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:56.369
If these resources are limited enough, it
is very likely that one or both of the saplings

00:09:56.369 --> 00:10:01.430
will die, rather than growing up into big,
strong trees that can survive well, even with

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:02.430
competition.

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:07.240
This is why I always highly recommend that
people NOT learn two closely related languages

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:08.529
at the same time.

00:10:08.529 --> 00:10:10.180
Spanish and Italian at the same time?

00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:11.180
Nope.

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:12.180
Polish and Czech.

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:13.180
Not a good idea.

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:14.199
Dutch and German?

00:10:14.199 --> 00:10:15.199
Not recommended.

00:10:15.199 --> 00:10:19.470
You’ll experience massive interference and
confusion throughout the learning process.

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:23.860
Before we go, I want to talk about another
type of interference between languages, which

00:10:23.860 --> 00:10:26.939
can get in your way even if you know both
languages well.

00:10:26.939 --> 00:10:28.480
I’ll call this interference “blocking”.

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:30.570
I’ll illustrate with a brief story:

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:35.709
I once knew an American girl who had moved
to Italy after living in Spain for three years.

00:10:35.709 --> 00:10:40.110
Upon arriving in Italy, she was totally fluent
in Spanish, and had no problems using it whatsoever.

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:43.029
When I met her, she had been living in Italy
for one year.

00:10:43.029 --> 00:10:47.389
Italian was her main language learning priority,
and she focused on learning it every single

00:10:47.389 --> 00:10:48.389
day.

00:10:48.389 --> 00:10:51.290
At the time we met, I would say she spoke
fluent Italian.

00:10:51.290 --> 00:10:55.149
One time, she told me the story of how she
recently met some Spanish tourists in Rome.

00:10:55.149 --> 00:10:58.740
Even though she hadn’t spoken Spanish in
a long time, she was confident in her skills,

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:01.600
and decided to strike up a conversation with
them.

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:07.290
Much to her surprise, when she opened her
mouth, no Spanish came out!

00:11:07.290 --> 00:11:09.769
Instead, all she could speak was Italian!

00:11:09.769 --> 00:11:15.220
In that moment, she said it was if she was
trying to dig through her brain for the Spanish

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:19.660
she was certain she knew, but all she could
find was Italian.

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:25.829
Somehow, her more recent and ready Italian
skills were blocking her Spanish skills!

00:11:25.829 --> 00:11:27.899
This has happened to me too, from time to
time.

00:11:27.899 --> 00:11:32.759
As a language learner, it can be quite a shocking
and uncomfortable experience, and is definitely

00:11:32.759 --> 00:11:35.620
one of the stranger forms of interference
one can encounter.

00:11:35.620 --> 00:11:41.589
Luckily, I can confidently say that this kind
of interference can definitely be overcome.

00:11:41.589 --> 00:11:46.189
In the above story, my friend was unable to
speak Spanish because her Italian knowledge

00:11:46.189 --> 00:11:48.929
was so much fresher in her mind.

00:11:48.929 --> 00:11:53.209
In fact, it had been more than a year since
she had spoken any Spanish at all.

00:11:53.209 --> 00:11:56.920
So the problem clearly is lack of practice.

00:11:56.920 --> 00:12:01.199
Even if you know two languages in a family
well, if you speak one and ignore the other

00:12:01.199 --> 00:12:05.629
for a long time, this “blocking” is sometimes
likely to happen.

00:12:05.629 --> 00:12:06.850
The solution?

00:12:06.850 --> 00:12:10.649
Make an effort to practice both languages,
as regularly as you can.

00:12:10.649 --> 00:12:15.910
If, say, your dominant language - given your
life choices and circumstances - is Spanish

00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:21.610
but you don’t want to lose your fluent Portuguese,
make sure you spend at least 10-20 minutes

00:12:21.610 --> 00:12:23.269
every day with Portuguese.

00:12:23.269 --> 00:12:28.649
You can do this in a variety of ways, be it
listening, reading, or even having a pretend

00:12:28.649 --> 00:12:31.350
conversation in Portuguese with yourself!

00:12:31.350 --> 00:12:36.800
Best would be to hop on a Skype call or maybe
text chat Whatsapp to keep your Portuguese

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:38.170
capacities alive.

00:12:38.170 --> 00:12:43.430
Now, before I finish, I wanted to share a
couple of other tips and make you aware of

00:12:43.430 --> 00:12:46.769
certain “traps” you may want to avoid.

00:12:46.769 --> 00:12:51.610
First, though learning one language in a family
is highly likely to make learning another

00:12:51.610 --> 00:12:54.910
language in that family easier, it is not
always the case.

00:12:54.910 --> 00:13:00.769
At the very least, certain structural features
of the language can be different enough to

00:13:00.769 --> 00:13:02.279
make learning harder.

00:13:02.279 --> 00:13:07.209
For example, though French and Spanish are
both Romance languages, French pronunciation

00:13:07.209 --> 00:13:09.779
is extremely different from Spanish pronunciation.

00:13:09.779 --> 00:13:15.600
So if you know Spanish and expect to learn
French pronunciation quickly and easily, you

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:16.980
may be sorely mistaken.

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:21.009
And another thing you may encounter when learning
multiple languages within a family is a decline

00:13:21.009 --> 00:13:23.959
in interest as you learn more languages.

00:13:23.959 --> 00:13:27.980
Since you’ve already mastered one or more
languages within the group, gaining knowledge

00:13:27.980 --> 00:13:31.980
of other similar languages may seem less challenging
or exciting to you.

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:36.610
In certain cases, this could demotivate you
and cause you to stop learning the new language.

00:13:36.610 --> 00:13:40.649
That’s what happened to me when I started
learning Romanian after learning French, Spanish,

00:13:40.649 --> 00:13:41.999
and Portuguese.

00:13:41.999 --> 00:13:46.610
Since Romanian would have been my fourth Romance
language, it didn’t seem as exciting or

00:13:46.610 --> 00:13:49.749
exotic as many of the other languages I had
my eye on.

00:13:49.749 --> 00:13:54.839
To make matters worse, I had no connections
with Romanian people at the time, so I wasn’t

00:13:54.839 --> 00:13:58.220
particularly motivated to find a way to learn
it.

00:13:58.220 --> 00:14:02.480
So to wrap it up, languages are grouped in
families.

00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:05.569
Languages of the same family share similar
traits.

00:14:05.569 --> 00:14:10.769
Learning one language within a family means
that in most cases, it will be easier to learn

00:14:10.769 --> 00:14:12.850
other languages in that family, as well.

00:14:12.850 --> 00:14:17.339
For this reason, if you want to become a polyglot,
it can often be a good idea to learn multiple

00:14:17.339 --> 00:14:21.149
languages in the same family before moving
on to other ones.

00:14:21.149 --> 00:14:25.540
There are downsides to this however, so if
you want to learn multiple languages in this

00:14:25.540 --> 00:14:28.420
way, you should follow certain strategies.

00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:32.899
For example, I highly recommend you learn
a language to fluency (B2 or even better,

00:14:32.899 --> 00:14:36.019
C1) before moving on to other languages within
that group.

00:14:36.019 --> 00:14:39.829
Don’t make the mistake of trying to learn
two close languages at the same time, or you

00:14:39.829 --> 00:14:41.180
will likely confuse them.

00:14:41.180 --> 00:14:46.239
Even with the right strategy and right timing,
a certain amount of interference - be it syntax,

00:14:46.239 --> 00:14:49.209
pronunciation, or vocabulary - is inevitable.

00:14:49.209 --> 00:14:52.040
This problem, however, can be easily solved.

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:57.130
The more you learn and speak two similar languages,
the better you will be able to keep them separate.

00:14:57.130 --> 00:15:02.790
If you’re in this situation, make sure you
use both languages on a daily or weekly basis,

00:15:02.790 --> 00:15:08.929
and make sure you invest some time in clearly
distinguishing and practicing the two different

00:15:08.929 --> 00:15:09.929
sound systems.

